Repro:
  In src/core/preflight_guard.rs, the builtin rule `builtin:rm_rf_root` uses pattern `*<destructive-recursive-delete> /*` (the literal three-token destructive command followed by the root slash). The matcher in src/core/tripwire.rs::glob_match treats `*` as "zero or more characters", so any command string that contains the literal substring of that destructive token sequence is flagged as a forbidden invocation.

  Quick check (works today against the public matcher):

      use ee::core::tripwire::glob_match;
      let rule = "*<destructive-recursive-delete> /*";  // see preflight_guard rule
      assert!(glob_match(rule, "git log --grep=\"<destructive-recursive-delete> /\""));
      assert!(glob_match(rule, "echo do not <destructive-recursive-delete> / blindly"));
      // and a contrived word-boundary false positive:
      assert!(glob_match(rule, "confirm -rf /var/cache"));

Expected: only an actual destructive invocation (the command starting with the destructive recursive delete at a token boundary) matches the halt rule. A quoted argument that mentions the destructive substring should NOT halt the caller.

Actual: any command whose flat string contains the destructive substring halts with exit 7 and cites the rm_rf_root rule. The third example above (where `confirm` ends with the literal two letters of the destructive command) is a contrived but real false positive that confirms the matcher does not respect word boundaries.

Evidence-line:
  src/core/preflight_guard.rs:267-272 (rm_rf_root rule pattern)
  src/core/tripwire.rs:247-296 (glob_match / glob_match_bytes)
  tests/preflight_guard.rs:34-65 ("agents_md_forbidden_actions_halt_with_exit_seven") only tests true-positive commands; no false-positive coverage.

Suggested fix: tighten the pattern to require word-boundary positions (e.g., anchor on `^<destr> /*` and on `* <destr> /*` separately), or upgrade the matcher to a tiny word-boundary-aware glob, or split incoming command on shell tokens before matching. Add false-positive tests that assert the three commands above stay at exit 0.

# Pin the test-event schema

## Why

Every plan-space revision in rounds 1-3 referenced \`ee.test_event.v1\` log events with a specific \`kind\` field and a kind-specific payload (e.g., \`{kind: 'pack_assembly_profile', ...}\`, \`{kind: 'audit_query', ...}\`, etc.). The producer side is \`src/obs/test_log.rs\` (J1, closed).

But there's no schema file that pins the union of allowed \`kind\` values. As more beads land, the union grows; without a schema gate, log consumers can't validate logs against a contract.

This bead pins the schema.

## Acceptance

### File: docs/schemas/test_event_v1.json (canonical)

JSON Schema draft-07:

\`\`\`json
{
  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#\",
  \"$id\": \"docs/schemas/test_event_v1.json\",
  \"title\": \"ee.test_event.v1\",
  \"type\": \"object\",
  \"required\": [\"schema\", \"kind\"],
  \"properties\": {
    \"schema\": { \"const\": \"ee.test_event.v1\" },
    \"kind\": {
      \"type\": \"string\",
      \"description\": \"The category of test event. Each kind has a kind-specific payload schema in oneOf.\"
    },
    \"emitted_at\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"format\": \"date-time\" },
    \"test_id\": { \"type\": \"string\" }
  },
  \"oneOf\": [
    { \"$ref\": \"#/definitions/event_pack_hash_components\" },
    { \"$ref\": \"#/definitions/event_audit_query\" },
    { \"$ref\": \"#/definitions/event_validity_filter\" },
    { \"$ref\": \"#/definitions/event_redaction_apply\" },
    { \"$ref\": \"#/definitions/event_clustering\" },
    { \"$ref\": \"#/definitions/event_level_transition\" },
    { \"$ref\": \"#/definitions/event_handoff_resume\" },
    { \"$ref\": \"#/definitions/event_renderer_parity\" },
    { \"$ref\": \"#/definitions/event_format_render\" },
    { \"$ref\": \"#/definitions/event_search_source_mode\" },
    { \"$ref\": \"#/definitions/event_lab_capture\" },
    { \"$ref\": \"#/definitions/event_lab_replay\" },
    { \"$ref\": \"#/definitions/event_lab_counterfactual\" },
    { \"$ref\": \"#/definitions/event_conformal\" },
    { \"$ref\": \"#/definitions/event_bayesian_update\" },
    { \"$ref\": \"#/definitions/event_causal_credit\" },
    { \"$ref\": \"#/definitions/event_simd_bm25\" },
    { \"$ref\": \"#/definitions/event_cusum\" },
    { \"$ref\": \"#/definitions/event_mi_dedup\" },
    { \"$ref\": \"#/definitions/event_volatile_strip\" },
    { \"$ref\": \"#/definitions/event_seed_label_fork\" },
    { \"$ref\": \"#/definitions/event_severity_audit\" },
    /* ... add per-kind definitions as beads land */
  ],
  \"definitions\": { /* per-kind payload schemas */ }
}
\`\`\`

Each \`definitions/event_<kind>\` pins the shape an emission of that kind must take. Adding a new kind requires updating this schema in the same PR.

### CI gate (\`tests/test_event_schema_gate.rs\`)

\`\`\`rust
#[test]
fn every_emitted_test_event_validates_against_schema() -> TestResult {
    let schema = load_schema(\"docs/schemas/test_event_v1.json\")?;
    let mut errors = Vec::new();
    for log_path in glob(\"tests/logs/active/*.jsonl\")? {
        for (line_no, line) in read_lines(log_path)?.enumerate() {
            let value: Value = serde_json::from_str(&line)?;
            if let Err(errs) = jsonschema::validate(&schema, &value) {
                errors.push(format!(\"{}:{} {:?}\", log_path, line_no, errs));
            }
        }
    }
    assert!(errors.is_empty(), \"{} log lines failed schema: {errors:#?}\", errors.len());
    Ok(())
}
\`\`\`

CI runs this after every test pass against the active log directory.

### Discovery gate (\`tests/test_event_kind_inventory.rs\`)

\`\`\`rust
#[test]
fn every_kind_in_schema_appears_in_at_least_one_emission_site() -> TestResult {
    // Parse schema; extract kind values from oneOf branches.
    let schema = load_schema(\"docs/schemas/test_event_v1.json\")?;
    let kinds_in_schema: BTreeSet<String> = ...;

    // Grep src/ for emission sites; extract kind literals.
    let kinds_in_src: BTreeSet<String> = ...;

    let orphan_in_schema: Vec<_> = kinds_in_schema.difference(&kinds_in_src).collect();
    let orphan_in_src: Vec<_> = kinds_in_src.difference(&kinds_in_schema).collect();

    assert!(orphan_in_schema.is_empty(), \"schema defines kinds not emitted: {orphan_in_schema:?}\");
    assert!(orphan_in_src.is_empty(), \"src emits kinds not in schema: {orphan_in_src:?}\");
    Ok(())
}
\`\`\`

### Logging event (meta)

The schema gate itself emits an event:
\`\`\`json
{
  \"schema\": \"ee.test_event.v1\",
  \"kind\": \"schema_gate\",
  \"target_schema\": \"ee.test_event.v1\",
  \"log_lines_checked\": <usize>,
  \"kinds_observed\": [\"pack_hash_components\", ...],
  \"orphans_in_schema\": [],
  \"orphans_in_src\": []
}
\`\`\`

(This is itself a registered \`kind\`.)

## Depends on

- J1 (\`bd-17c65.10.1\`) — the producer side, closed.
- K2 (\`bd-17c65.11.2\`) — per-envelope JSON Schema infrastructure.

## Blocks

Every bead that emits a test event implicitly composes with this. When this gate lands, those beads' acceptance gates compose automatically.

## What this bead does NOT cover

- Runtime log emission (the producer already exists in src/obs/test_log.rs).
- Log aggregation / shipping to external systems.
- Performance of the schema validator itself.

Just: the contract that says \"a test event MUST have a kind from this enumeration, and the payload MUST match the kind's schema\".

