# IdeaWizard pass by DarkDuck (2026-05-16) — Agent Workload Flight Recorder + Replay Lab

## Why this epic exists

The current backlog already covers most obvious swarm-scale ideas: RCH capability reconciliation, verification reuse, single-flight, context hotset prewarm, L2 pack cache, streaming context output, NUMA graph snapshots, mesh optionality, and aggressive search/write hot-path optimization. The highest-value gap is not another cache. It is a way to know how real agents actually use `ee` and replay those workloads deterministically without leaking source content, mail bodies, prompts, secrets, or raw memories.

Today we can benchmark synthetic operations and inspect pack ledgers, but we do not have a redacted, local-first flight recorder that says: an agent ran these command shapes, hit these degraded codes, consumed this many bytes/tokens, selected these memory IDs, then later produced this outcome. Without that loop, future performance and UX work risks optimizing toy fixtures instead of the actual agent harness path.

This epic adds an opt-in, redaction-first workload telemetry loop:

1. record command-shape telemetry for agent-facing `ee` calls without raw content;
2. convert traces into deterministic replay fixtures and perf budgets;
3. diagnose prompt-budget waste across multi-command agent workflows;
4. expose counterfactual "why was this not selected?" answers for missed context; and
5. run synthetic 64-agent swarm playback from recorded traces.

The result is a project-level control loop: live agent behavior -> redacted evidence -> deterministic replay -> regression budgets -> better context/search/pack decisions.

## Phase 2: 30 ideas considered, best 5 retained

Raw ideas considered:
1. redacted command flight recorder for every agent-facing `ee` call;
2. deterministic replay harness from recorded traces;
3. prompt-budget planner across context/search/why/handoff sequences;
4. counterfactual why-not-selected diagnostics for context packs;
5. 64-agent swarm playback lab from real traces;
6. pack diet report for redundant JSON/token overhead;
7. evidence freshness SLA for verification and coordination claims;
8. command latency flamegraph emitted as `ee.perf.v1`;
9. schema drift heatmap by command surface;
10. per-agent error-recovery success tracker;
11. outcome attribution from Beads close comments to pack IDs;
12. redaction canary corpus for flight telemetry;
13. operator cockpit for degraded-code trends;
14. `ee doctor --agent-harness codex|claude` presets;
15. command sequence recommender for common tasks;
16. noisy-memory quarantine based on repeated why-not evidence;
17. sparse-workspace onboarding fixture generator;
18. content-free similarity sketches for missed-memory clustering;
19. trace-to-benchmark promotion workflow;
20. cross-platform trace hash comparison;
21. agent harness contract fuzzer;
22. stale coordination evidence budget and expiry model;
23. local privacy audit for every support/flight artifact;
24. task-classifier confusion matrix from pack outcomes;
25. context retry policy hints when semantic search is degraded;
26. deterministic failure injection for mail/beads/rch absent states;
27. replayable support bundle minimizer;
28. telemetry retention and quota manager;
29. per-command redaction proof snapshot; and
30. trace import/export for offline regression review.

The best five became child beads because they form a complete, pragmatic feedback loop and do not duplicate existing cache/mesh/RCH work.

## Phase 3: next 10 ideas and disposition

The next ten were: pack diet report, evidence freshness SLA, latency flamegraph, schema drift heatmap, error-recovery success tracker, Beads outcome attribution, redaction canary corpus, degraded-code trend cockpit, harness-specific doctor presets, and command sequence recommender. They are not filed as separate top-level beads now. They are folded into child acceptance criteria where they are load-bearing, or left as explicit future follow-ups once the recorder/replay substrate exists.

## Phase 4: overlap decisions

- Does not duplicate `bd-1zb7k.17`: that epic handles RCH/Codex capability, duplicate work, hotset prewarm, and pack-quality feedback. This epic supplies the content-safe workload evidence those surfaces can later consume.
- Does not duplicate `bd-6boyo`: verification broker traces are about RCH/test evidence. This epic traces `ee` command workloads and agent-facing JSON/token behavior.
- Does not duplicate `bd-1prrl`: Swarm-X contains concrete hot-path accelerators. This epic provides workload replay and regression evidence for those accelerators.
- Does not duplicate `bd-3gutc`: alien-artifact responsiveness owns major performance subsystems. This epic is the measurement and replay loop that proves they help real agents.
- Does not create a daemon-first dependency. The recorder is opt-in, local-first, bounded, and every core command remains one-shot CLI usable.

## Closure criteria

This epic closes when all five child beads ship with unit tests, deterministic replay fixtures, no-mock e2e scripts with structured logs, failure-mode fixtures for any new degraded codes, docs for agent harness authors, and RCH-verified Rust gates. The redaction contract is non-negotiable: traces must never store raw query text, raw memory content, raw mail bodies, secrets, environment dumps, or full file listings.

