description:
Repro:
  src/db/mod.rs:8696 defines `generate_audit_id` as:

      pub fn generate_audit_id() -> String {
          let now = Utc::now();
          let nanos = now.timestamp_nanos_opt().unwrap_or(0);
          format!("audit_{:026x}", nanos as u128)
      }

  The id is a pure function of the current nanosecond. Two audit-log
  inserts that fall in the same nanosecond produce the same id, which
  collides on the audit_log PRIMARY KEY and the second insert fails with
  a constraint violation. This is reachable from any tight loop that
  emits audit rows: the recorder import path inserting many events,
  bulk JSONL import, or any test/benchmark that calls insert_memory_audited
  in a row.

  A demonstration:

      use ee::db::generate_audit_id;
      let a = generate_audit_id();
      let b = generate_audit_id();
      // On a fast machine these can be identical.
      // Even if not always, *eventually* a high-throughput call site collides.

Expected: every call returns a unique id even within a single nanosecond.

Actual: id is a deterministic function of `now.timestamp_nanos_opt()` only — no counter, no randomness, no per-process disambiguator. Same-nanosecond callers get colliding ids.

Aggravating cases:
  - `unwrap_or(0)` on `timestamp_nanos_opt()`: if the system clock is ever out
    of the ~1677..=2262 valid range, every call returns `audit_{0:026x}` =
    `audit_00000000000000000000000000`, instantly colliding with itself.
  - `nanos as u128` casts an `i64` to `u128`. For valid current timestamps the
    cast is fine, but the cast is unchecked — if `timestamp_nanos_opt` ever
    returned a negative i64 (it can't today, but the contract is loose), the
    cast would sign-extend and produce an absurd 32-hex-digit string that
    overflows the `{:026x}` width and breaks the GLOB constraint
    `audit_log.id GLOB 'audit_*' AND length(...) = 32`.

Evidence-line:
  src/db/mod.rs:8696-8700 (generate_audit_id)
  src/db/mod.rs (audit_log table CHECK constraint on id length)

Suggested fix: append a small process-local counter or a short random suffix
to disambiguate within a nanosecond, e.g.:

      use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicU64, Ordering};
      static COUNTER: AtomicU64 = AtomicU64::new(0);

      pub fn generate_audit_id() -> String {
          let nanos = Utc::now().timestamp_nanos_opt().unwrap_or(0).max(0) as u128;
          let seq = COUNTER.fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed) as u128 & 0xFFFF;
          format!("audit_{:022x}{:04x}", nanos, seq)  // 22 + 4 = 26 hex chars
      }

  Or switch to UUIDv7 (already a project dep — see core/recorder.rs uses
  `uuid::Uuid::now_v7()` for the same purpose). UUIDv7 is purpose-built for
  monotonic, collision-resistant ids in time order.

